# Separate supervised current249 read-only capture

This HOST packet supplies the missing precondition of the five-stage R61 HIL operator. It is not a device grant. Historical bootable/FROZEN.json, operator-r61/ENVELOPE-FROZEN.json and PLAN.md are unchanged.

Only stage resident-capture exists in this supervisor. The narrow scope is PICO_R61_READONLY_CURRENT249_ONCE. Start only through ARGV-resident-capture.json with a fresh canonical Root stage grant, fresh resident route/device metadata and a fresh output. The grant template is disabled, expires=0 and has no presumed physical counter (expected_physical_keys=null). Literal historical counters are rejected before the first command. The old values 25/26 in HOST tests are fixtures, never current measurements.

The first ENQ obtains and validates one complete current249 status (BUILD249A92DE, valid E6CFC3B8, idle closed resident/foreground, exact framing). Its actual PHYSICAL_KEYS and BOOTS/RESET_REASON tuple are saved as BASELINE.json alongside the unmodified baseline/status-001.raw. An exact uint32 counter is required. A second Resident instance is seeded with both observed values before admit/status or any getter. Every following status verifies both, including immediately before command-mode entry, before every getter and at the end. Final IP and settings must also match the first discovery status. A change causes HOLD; no reset, retry, transition or implicit recovery follows.

The source-qualified snapshot parser, framing, fixed getter whitelist, zero-state predicate and complete capture body after entry are copied exactly from frozen R2. The three clock/parser helpers are byte-exact. Only capture entry now requires the freshly observed epoch/counter; the unsupervised old CLI was omitted. A single command-mode US acknowledgement permits seven fixed getters: identity, reg, stk, hout, pwd, alarm, date. Identity verifies the actual UID. The calendar is bracketed with the original continuous host clock helper. Normal transaction has 12 status frames, one US, seven getters, 50 transmitted bytes; budget remains <=128TX, <=262144RX, <=120s. No RAW !, X, DFU, reset, reinit, Flash/SD/APP access, key injection or date setter is present in the allowed commands.

The typed current-state.json retains observed counter and epoch, settings, registers, stack, program bytes, IP, directory, alarm, identity, continuous-clock bracket and current_zero_data_losslessly_rebootable. Configured RTC or nonzero data may be captured but never gains RAM/reboot permission: RAM_launch_or_clock_return_GO remains false. Root must independently inspect and pin this new output and use its actual counter/epoch for any later resident-rom grant; the old R61 return prerequisite RTC_SET=0/zero transient data remains unchanged.

LeasePort, live canonical-byte/hash guards, per-native-operation checks, owned descriptor cleanup, owned process-group cancellation and durable argv/PID/EXIT supervision are reused from reviewed R61. Only the read-only scope string and one-stage routing differ. Closing owned handles remains permitted after withdrawal. No other device actor, tool, old lease or status is reused.

HOST checks use explicitly inert saved wire/typed-state fixtures and a fake clock, never current-device evidence. Counts0/25/26/731/uint32max succeed. Changed counter and epoch are rejected at every one of eleven subsequent status positions; a change immediately after discovery is rejected before US/getters. Wrong UID/CRC, changed settings/IP, historical grant counters and revocation are rejected. Configured/nonzero snapshots still deny RAM GO. Copied native-I/O revocation and owned-child tests pass. Early inert fixture and padded-decimal-IP audit failures are retained separately and repaired before freezing. New hardware/ARM/MPFR/SDK/Git/canonical writes: zero.
